{"edition":"Diels, Die Fragmente der Vorsokratiker, 3rd ed. 1912","book":"6a3f0220661123baf6fe9d4e","chapters":[[32,44]],"threshold":{"in":[0.12,0.84],"hop":0.88,"judgeTop":200},"entries":[["32 A 1","testimony",322,"Diogenes Laertius Viii 84, 85","Philolaus of Croton was a Pythagorean. Plato wrote to Dion to purchase the Pythagorean books from him. (He died when he was thought to be attempting to seize a tyranny. We have an epigram for him:) I ",52,46,180],["32 A 1a","testimony",322,"Plato Phaedo 61 E","\"I also heard Philolaus,\" said Cebes, \"when he was living among us\" . See B 15.",3,3,-348],["32 A 2","testimony",323,"Diogenes Laertius ix 38","Apollodorus of Cyzicus also says that Democritus met with Philolaus.",15,15,1472],["32 A 3","testimony",323,"Cicero On the Orator iii 34, 139","or Philolaus instructed Archytas of Tarentum.",0,0,null],["32 A 4","testimony",323,"Diogenes Laertius viii 46","For they were the last of the Pythagoreans, whom Aristoxenus also saw: Xenophilus of Chalcidice from Thrace, Phanton of Phlius, and Echecrates, Diocles, and Polymnastus, also from Phlius. They were st",8,7,1550],["32 A 4a","testimony",323,"Plutarch On the Sign of Socrates 13 page 583 a","For when the associations of the Pythagoreans in the cities were cast out, having been overcome by faction, the followers of Cylon surrounded with fire the house in Metapontum where those still united",2,2,-348],["32 A 5","testimony",323,"Diogenes Laertius iii 6","Then, having become twenty-eight years old, as Hermodorus says, Plato withdrew to Megara to see Euclides with some other followers of Socrates. Then he went away to Cyrene to see Theodorus the mathema",0,0,null],["32 A 6","testimony",323,"Vitruvius i 1, 16","To those indeed to whom nature has granted so much skill, sharpness, and memory that they can have geometry, astronomy, music, and the other disciplines thoroughly known... these however are rarely fo",6,6,-14],["32 A 7","testimony",323,"Athenaeus iv 184 e","And many of the